usb-net: report the link's state in OID_GEN_MEDIA_CONNECT_STATUS

The RNDIS model sets media_state to connected when it is realized and
never changes it, so `set_link <id> off` drops the frames but the
adapter still answers OID_GEN_MEDIA_CONNECT_STATUS with "connected". A
guest that watches the link to notice a cable pulled and put back --
releasing its address so traffic moves to another interface, and asking
DHCP again when the cable returns -- has nothing to see.

Give the NIC a link_status_changed that sets media_state from
nc->link_down, as other NIC models do for their link registers.

Found by the InferNode bare-metal harness: etherusb's link watcher
polls this OID, and set_link through QMP had no effect on it.

--- a/hw/usb/dev-network.c	2026-10-08 10:09:38.095714981 +0700
+++ b/hw/usb/dev-network.c	2026-10-08 10:09:38.143713063 +0700
@@ -1334,6 +1334,14 @@
     return size;
 }
 
+static void usbnet_link_status_changed(NetClientState *nc)
+{
+    USBNetState *s = qemu_get_nic_opaque(nc);
+
+    /* NDIS_MEDIA_STATE_DISCONNECTED (1) while the link is down */
+    s->media_state = nc->link_down ? 1 : 0;
+}
+
 static void usbnet_cleanup(NetClientState *nc)
 {
     USBNetState *s = qemu_get_nic_opaque(nc);
@@ -1355,6 +1363,7 @@
     .size = sizeof(NICState),
     .receive = usbnet_receive,
     .cleanup = usbnet_cleanup,
+    .link_status_changed = usbnet_link_status_changed,
 };
 
 static void usb_net_realize(USBDevice *dev, Error **errp)
